-- title:
--   Λ-eigenspace meets the span of (̂ t-Λ(t))-images trivially
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $J$ be an abelian group equipped with an action of the abstract Hecke algebra $\mathtt{HeckeAlgOne} = \mathbb{Z}[X_i : i \in \mathrm{Primes} \sqcup \mathbb{N}]$, and suppose the Tate module $T_p J$ — the group of sequences $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $J$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$, a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-module — is finite and free over $\mathbb{Z}_p$. Write $\hat t \in \mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{Z}_p}(T_pJ)$ for the operator induced by $t \in \mathtt{HeckeAlgOne}$, and $\hat t_{\mathbb{Q}_p}$ for its base change to $\mathbb{Q}_p \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} T_pJ$; let $A_1$ be the $\mathbb{Q}_p$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{Q}_p}(\mathbb{Q}_p \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} T_pJ)$ generated by all $\hat t_{\mathbb{Q}_p}$. Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and $\Lambda \colon A_1 \to K$ a ring homomorphism whose restriction to the scalars $c \in \mathbb{Z}_p \subseteq \mathbb{Q}_p$ is the structure map $\mathbb{Z}_p \to K$. Let $G \subseteq \mathtt{HeckeAlgOne}$ be a set such that: (i) for every $w$ in the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra generated by $G$ there is a non-zero $P \in \mathbb{Z}[X]$ whose image in $\mathbb{Q}[X]$ is separable and with $P(\hat w) = 0$ on $T_pJ$; and (ii) for every $t$ there are $s, u$ in that subalgebra with $\Lambda(\hat s_{\mathbb{Q}_p}) \ne 0$ and $\hat s \hat t = \hat u$ on $T_pJ$. Then, inside $K \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} T_pJ$, $$\Bigl(\bigcap_{t} \ker\bigl(\hat t_K - \Lambda(\hat t_{\mathbb{Q}_p})\bigr)\Bigr) \cap \mathrm{span}_K\bigl\{\hat t_K y - \Lambda(\hat t_{\mathbb{Q}_p}) y : t \in \mathtt{HeckeAlgOne},\ y \in K \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_p} T_pJ\bigr\} = 0.$$
--
--   This is the second half of the statement that, at a character $\Lambda$ of the Hecke algebra, the Tate module over $K$ splits as the $\Lambda$-eigenspace plus the submodule spanned by the images of the operators $\hat t - \Lambda(t)$: here the two pieces are shown to intersect in zero, under the hypotheses that the distinguished ("good") Hecke operators are annihilated by polynomials separable over $\mathbb{Q}$ and that every Hecke operator becomes, after multiplication by a good operator not killed by $\Lambda$, again a good operator. It is stated for an arbitrary Hecke module $J$ and is applied with $J = J_1(M)$ at a primitive form by [`CuspForm.IsPrimitiveForm.iInf_ker_hecke_sub_ne_bot_and_inf_span_eq_bot_tateModule_jOne`](thm.html#CuspForm.IsPrimitiveForm.iInf_ker_hecke_sub_ne_bot_and_inf_span_eq_bot_tateModule_jOne).
